WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.762
Harvest-now, decrypt-later risk should be communicated without panic.

00:00:03.762 --> 00:00:07.883
Leadership does not need speculative countdowns or dramatic claims.

00:00:07.883 --> 00:00:14.292
Leaders need to understand that some encrypted data may remain valuable long after collection,

00:00:14.292 --> 00:00:19.327
and that future advances could weaken assumptions behind certain public-key protections.

00:00:19.327 --> 00:00:24.363
The useful message is practical: identify long-lived sensitive data, understand dependencies,

00:00:24.363 --> 00:00:29.399
prioritize action, and make migration possible before urgency forces rushed decisions.

00:00:29.399 --> 00:00:32.604
Governance turns that message into a program.

00:00:32.604 --> 00:00:38.097
Risk registers can track systems with long confidentiality lifetimes, exposed data flows,

00:00:38.097 --> 00:00:42.217
unresolved cryptographic dependencies, vendor blockers, and accepted residual risk.

00:00:42.217 --> 00:00:46.795
Migration roadmaps can define phases for inventory, pilots, procurement updates,

00:00:46.795 --> 00:00:50.915
standards alignment, vendor coordination, testing, staged deployment, and monitoring.

00:00:50.915 --> 00:00:55.036
Policy updates can describe approved cryptographic approaches, retention expectations,

00:00:55.036 --> 00:00:57.324
exception handling, and documentation requirements.

00:00:57.324 --> 00:01:01.445
Procurement and vendor management are part of the work.

00:01:01.445 --> 00:01:05.565
Organizations should ask suppliers about cryptographic dependencies, PQC readiness,

00:01:05.565 --> 00:01:09.227
roadmap assumptions, supported protocols, certificate strategy, library support,

00:01:09.227 --> 00:01:12.890
hardware constraints, monitoring capabilities, and customer configuration options.

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:17.010
Vendor answers should be treated as inputs, not guarantees.

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:19.299
Teams still need testing, documentation,

00:01:19.299 --> 00:01:25.250
and contingency plans when roadmap dates shift or support arrives unevenly across products.

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:28.455
Program planning should document assumptions and uncertainty.

00:01:28.455 --> 00:01:33.948
Nobody can responsibly promise an exact future threat date or claim that

00:01:33.948 --> 00:01:37.153
a single technology eliminates all cryptographic risk.

00:01:37.153 --> 00:01:41.731
Good communication explains priorities, owners, blockers, decisions needed, funding needs,

00:01:41.731 --> 00:01:44.020
dependency risks, and measurable progress.

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:45.393
This keeps harvest-now,

00:01:45.393 --> 00:01:50.429
decrypt-later planning grounded in governance and engineering rather than fear, slogans,

00:01:50.429 --> 00:01:52.260
or abstract cryptographic theory.
